# Code-comment register: an English CoNLL-U catalog.
#
# WHAT THIS IS. A register profile for code comments and doc comments. The
# question it answers is not "was this comment written by an LLM?" -- a 5-20
# word span carries far too little signal for that, and the answer would be
# unfalsifiable anyway -- but "is this comment written in the register this
# codebase expects?". Register is a property of the text, so it can be
# measured, cited, and disagreed with.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE. Published slop detectors (EQ-Bench's Slop Score,
# slop-forensics) score a bag of words: they report that some corpus overuses
# `seamlessly`. This catalog conditions the same counts on GRAMMAR, so the
# report is instead "the ADV->VERB advmod slot is filled by `seamlessly` at N
# times the rate of the comparison corpus, while the NOUN->ADJ amod slot
# shifts toward `robust`". That is the collostructional method
# `patterns/sketch-grammar.lx` and `patterns/collexeme.lx` already implement
# for Japanese, and this is its English instance.
#
# The slots are DELIBERATELY UNRESTRICTED. `evaluative_amod` captures every
# adjective, not a hand-written list of marketing adjectives, because a baked
# lexicon assumes the conclusion -- and a fixed word list is the one part of
# this a plain regex already does. Which lemmas are distinctive is the
# measurement, not the input. `examples/comment_register.py` runs the keyness.
#
# BINDING. This catalog requires `dep.head` and `dep.rel`, so it binds against
# CoNLL-U input only and is refused by name against a morphology-only corpus.
# It declares no `analyzer:` block: the CoNLL-U reader's family is
# `pretokenized`, so a `vibrato` requirement would refuse every corpus this
# catalog exists to read.
#
# It declares no `examples:` either, and that is not an oversight.
# `lingrep-core/tests/suite/catalog_examples.rs` verifies every claim with
# `tokenize_japanese`; an English example would be tokenized as Japanese and
# the claim would fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the pattern.
# Executable claims for this catalog live in the harness that runs it.
#
# DEPENDENCY LABEL PORTABILITY. Two English parsers produce two label sets.
# stanza emits Universal Dependencies (`obj`, `obl`, `case`); spaCy's English
# models emit ClearNLP (`dobj`, `pobj`, `prep`). The patterns below are built
# on the labels the two schemes SHARE -- `amod`, `advmod`, `conj`, `nsubj` --
# which is where the register signal sits anyway. Where a divergent label is
# genuinely wanted, spell both terms in one bracket (`-[obj|dobj]->`) rather
# than assuming a parser; a constraint naming one scheme's label silently
# matches nothing under the other, which reads as a null result rather than as
# a configuration error.

defaults
  status: "experimental"
end

schema
  pattern layer: string required in {"narration", "evaluation", "structure", "denominator"}
  status: string required in {"experimental"}
end

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Narration: comments that tell a story about the code instead of stating a
# constraint on it. These are shapes, not words, which is why they survive a
# model upgrade that retires any particular adjective.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# "Ensuring that every cached entry is invalidated." A comment opening on a
# gerund narrates an activity; the engineering register opens on the thing
# being constrained. `^` anchors to the sentence start, so this fires on the
# opening of a comment rather than anywhere a gerund appears.
#
# TWO SPELLINGS, for the same reason the dependency labels below carry two.
# `VerbForm=Ger` is the UD spelling stanza emits. spaCy's English models do
# not use it: an `-ing` form is `VerbForm=Part` with `Tense=Pres`. Over
# one 1.8 M-token corpus, matching on `Ger` alone matched 0 occurrences
# where counting the construction by hand found 2,106. `Tense=Pres` is what
# keeps the second spelling from also taking a past participle, which is
# `VerbForm=Part` with `Tense=Past` and is a different construction.
key: [lemma]
layer: "narration"
pattern gerund_opening :=
  ^ [upos=VERB
     & (feat.VerbForm has Ger
        | (feat.VerbForm has Part & feat.Tense has Pres))].

# "A class that represents a bounded queue." The definitional copula opening,
# which restates the declaration the reader is already looking at. Keyed on
# the nominal the copula predicates over, because the bare surface of a
# multi-token match is a concatenation and reads as noise in a ranked table.
key: [$head.lemma]
layer: "narration"
pattern copular_definition :=
  ^ [upos=DET] [upos in {NOUN, PROPN, ADJ}]{0,2}
  (?<head> [upos in {NOUN, PROPN, ADJ}]) [lemma=be].

# "This method is responsible for validating the input." A docstring-generator
# template, and the highest-precision single string in the whole catalog.
key: [$frame.lemma]
layer: "narration"
pattern responsible_template :=
  [lemma=be] [upos=ADV]{0,2}
  (?<frame> [lemma in {responsible, intended, designed, meant, used}])
  [lemma in {for, to}].

# "This allows you to configure the retry budget." Second person addresses a
# reader being taught; a code comment is written for a maintainer who is
# already here. Keyed so the report names which verb carried it.
key: [$verb.lemma]
layer: "narration"
pattern reader_address :=
  (?<verb> [lemma in {allow, enable, let, help, permit}]) [lemma=you].

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evaluation: word sketches over the two modifier slots. Both are keyed, so
# `summarize` returns a collocation profile rather than a count, and both
# leave the modifier unconstrained so the distinctive fillers are discovered.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The adjective-noun sketch. English adjectival modifiers overwhelmingly
# precede their head, so one linear order covers the relation.
key: [$adj.lemma, $noun.lemma]
layer: "evaluation"
pattern evaluative_amod :=
  (?<adj> [upos=ADJ]) []{0,3} (?<noun> [upos in {NOUN, PROPN}])
  where $noun -[amod]-> $adj.

# The adverb-verb sketch, in the two linear orders English allows
# ("seamlessly handles" and "handles it seamlessly"). These are ONE relation
# written twice, not two findings: a capture inside an alternation branch
# binds only when that branch is taken, so `(A | B)` carrying captures on both
# sides is rejected as `WhereExclusiveCaptures`. Their shared `output_name`
# makes `summarize` aggregate them as one relation.
key: [$adv.lemma, $verb.lemma]
layer: "evaluation"
output_name: "softener_predicate"
pattern softener_before_predicate :=
  (?<adv> [upos=ADV]) []{0,3} (?<verb> [upos in {VERB, AUX}])
  where $verb -[advmod]-> $adv.

key: [$adv.lemma, $verb.lemma]
layer: "evaluation"
output_name: "softener_predicate"
pattern softener_after_predicate :=
  (?<verb> [upos in {VERB, AUX}]) []{0,3} (?<adv> [upos=ADV])
  where $verb -[advmod]-> $adv.

# "Handles all edge cases." Scope inflation: a universal quantifier claims a
# generality the annotated code rarely has, and unlike an adjective it is a
# checkable claim.
key: [$quantifier.lemma, $scope.lemma]
layer: "evaluation"
pattern universal_scope :=
  (?<quantifier> [upos=DET & lemma in {all, every, any, each}])
  [upos in {ADJ, VERB, NOUN}]{0,3}
  (?<scope> [upos in {NOUN, PROPN}]).

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Structure: cadence rather than vocabulary.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The rule of three -- "fast, reliable, and scalable". In UD and ClearNLP
# alike the first conjunct heads the later ones, so one capture governing two
# `conj` dependents is the whole tricolon regardless of where the commas fall.
key: [$first.lemma, $second.lemma, $third.lemma]
layer: "structure"
pattern tricolon :=
  (?<first> [upos in {ADJ, NOUN, VERB, ADV}]) []{0,5}
  (?<second> [upos in {ADJ, NOUN, VERB, ADV}]) []{0,5}
  (?<third> [upos in {ADJ, NOUN, VERB, ADV}])
  where $first -[conj]-> $second, $first -[conj]-> $third.

# "Not a cache, but an index." Bounded by intersection rather than by
# enumerating the gap widths: a length bound does not distribute over a
# concatenation containing a variable-length part, so `& []{3,12}` imposes it
# once instead of multiplying the alternatives out by hand.
layer: "structure"
pattern not_x_but_y :=
  ([lemma in {not, no, never}] []+ [lemma=but]) & []{3,12}.

# Dash asides, counted rather than judged. This is the weakest signal here
# and is reported as such -- it is a house-style tell, not a quality one, and
# the literature attributes the em dash to one vendor rather than to machine
# authorship in general.
#
# The ASCII spellings are here because without them a corpus that writes its
# asides as `--` scores zero on a construction it uses, which makes the
# measurement a question about typography rather than about the aside. Both
# are one PUNCT token under spaCy. `key: [text]` keeps the spellings apart in
# the report, so a reader can still separate the typographic habit from the
# construction: over three corpora the typographic gap was 42x and the
# construction gap 8.7x.
#
# THE NEIGHBOURS ARE THE CONSTRUCTION. An aside interrupts running text, so
# it has a word on each side; a rule drawn across a file does not. Without
# that constraint this pattern counted `;; --- hex helpers ----------` as
# thirteen asides, and a cleanup that deleted such banners registered as a
# change in how the corpus writes asides. It was a real finding about the
# wrong thing: `dash_aside` separated deleted Clojure comments from kept ones
# at q = 6.7e-06, entirely on `---`, while the em dash -- the construction --
# moved 3.6% the other way.
#
# `!(upos=PUNCT)` is the TRUE COMPLEMENT and not `upos!=PUNCT`, which would
# fail on a token carrying no `upos` and make this pattern match nothing at
# all against a corpus without part-of-speech annotation. Silently matching
# nothing is the failure mode `gerund_opening` already cost this catalog one
# whole finding to; over-counting is visible and a null result is not. The
# lookarounds are zero-width, so the definition stays exactly one token wide
# and `key: [text]` still projects the match's own spelling.
#
# Two narrowings follow and are intended. A dash opening a sentence has no
# left neighbour, so `preceded_by` fails and it is not counted. A dash beside
# other punctuation -- after a closing parenthesis, before a quotation mark --
# is not counted either; that costs about 5% of em-dash matches in this
# repository's prose and is the price of the rule being about neighbours
# rather than about a list of punctuation that may abut an aside.
key: [text]
layer: "structure"
pattern dash_aside :=
  preceded_by([!(upos=PUNCT)])
  [upos=PUNCT & text in {—, –, --, ---}]
  followed_by([!(upos=PUNCT)]).

# Nominalization density: a verbosity proxy that needs no reference text. A
# clause rewritten as a noun phrase says the same thing in more words.
key: [lemma]
layer: "structure"
pattern nominalization :=
  [upos in {NOUN, PROPN} & text ~ /(?:tion|ment|ity|ness|ance|ence)s?\z/].

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Denominator. Every rate above is per token, and this supplies the count.
# Keyed on lemma and part of speech it is also the plain unigram profile --
# the bag-of-words control that shows what the grammatical conditioning above
# actually buys over a slop word list.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

key: [lemma, upos]
layer: "denominator"
pattern token := [].
